      "latency_ms": 25156.9,
      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1181,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**, which means it is made up of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, how it works, and why it is important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. This means the electrons in the water molecule are not shared equally between the oxygen and the hydrogen atoms.\n*   **Oxygen** is more \"electronegative\" (it pulls electrons toward itself), giving it a partial negative charge.\n*   **Hydrogen** has a partial positive charge.\n*   This polarity allows water molecules to stick together (cohesion) and to other substances (adhesion), which is why water is a liquid and why it can dissolve so many things.\n\n### 2. Physical Properties\n*   **State:** At room temperature, water is a liquid. However, it can exist as a solid (ice) or a gas (steam) depending on the temperature.\n*   **Freezing and Boiling:** Water freezes at 0°C (32°F) and boils at 100°C (212°F).\n*   **Density:** Water is unique because it is most dense at 4°C. This means that when water freezes, it expands and becomes less dense, causing ice to float on liquid water. This is vital for life because it insulates lakes and oceans, allowing life to exist beneath the ice.\n*   **Solvent:** Water is often called the \"universal solvent\" because it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This makes it the primary transport medium in the human body and in nature.\n\n### 3. Abundance\nWater is the most abundant molecule on the surface of the Earth. It covers about 71% of the planet and makes up about 60% of the human body. It is found in oceans, rivers, lakes, glaciers, underground aquifers, and even in the atmosphere as clouds and rain.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance that is essential to all known forms of life."
